"Plots paired numeric observations to reveal association, clusters, and departures from a relationship.\n\nUse when: Two quantitative variables are measured on the same observational units. The form and spread of their association need inspection.\n\nAvoid when: Values are unpaired aggregates or the sample is too dense without aggregation.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal and vertical positions encode the two numeric variables. Each equal-sized point represents one observational unit.\n\nBuild: Match each x value to its correct y observation. Choose linear or justified transformed scales and label both units. Draw points with modest opacity and label only important observations outside marks.\n\nCheck: Check both variables use compatible inclusion criteria and time windows. Verify axes and transformations are explicit. Inspect how missing values, duplicates, and influential observations were handled.\n\nPitfalls: Association does not establish causation, even with a strong apparent trend. Overplotting can conceal density; use hexagonal bins or density contours instead of moving measured coordinates.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic package weight and shipping cost for 120 orders, with one point per package.\n\nAlternatives: Bubble chart, Hexbin plot, Regression plot."
